1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:09,450
now the last part about operating systems'
structures so if we look at the evolution

2
00:00:09,450 --> 00:00:15,960
of operating systems over time we can observe
that things got more and more complicated

3
00:00:15,960 --> 00:00:22,609
over time in the beginning operating systems
had a simple structure

4
00:00:22,609 --> 00:00:29,840
or so-called monolithic structure there was
no dual mode so basically all applications

5
00:00:29,840 --> 00:00:37,210
were directly interfacing with the
hardware for exchanging input or output of

6
00:00:37,210 --> 00:00:44,899
course this makes everything vulnerable to
wrong or malicious code but this was just

7
00:00:44,899 --> 00:00:46,920
the beginning of operating systems

8
00:00:46,920 --> 00:00:58,230
so from simple structures we then moved to
the so-called layered approach it's a modular

9
00:00:58,230 --> 00:01:05,070
approach in which you have these layers and
each layer maintains some data structure

10
00:01:05,070 --> 00:01:12,770
or set of functions and routines it makes
it easier to debug because you

11
00:01:12,770 --> 00:01:17,100
start having separate modules

12
00:01:17,100 --> 00:01:23,750
the problem here is that of circular
references so you might want to have a module

13
00:01:23,750 --> 00:01:29,310
that contains that CPU scheduler
and that would be above the module

14
00:01:29,310 --> 00:01:34,860
of the processes because first you need
to define what a process is what a running program is

15
00:01:34,860 --> 00:01:37,670
and that you can decide how you schedule them

16
00:01:37,670 --> 00:01:42,790
but on the other hand you might want to
have the module about processes that stays above

17
00:01:42,790 --> 00:01:50,049
the module about the CPU where you might have
also the scheduler because it's the process

18
00:01:50,049 --> 00:01:53,790
who uses the CPU not the CPU who
uses the process

19
00:01:53,790 --> 00:01:58,710
so it's hard to define and interconnect the
different modules if you need to keep them

20
00:01:58,710 --> 00:02:10,670
in this layer one on top of the other with
exclusive competence in each layer so we then

21
00:02:10,670 --> 00:02:18,440
moved to micro kernels the idea was to
keep the core part of the operating system

22
00:02:18,440 --> 00:02:22,280
as small as possible and move everything that
is not

23
00:02:22,280 --> 00:02:31,790
essential to the system to the user level program
it makes it smaller so it's also more easy

24
00:02:31,790 --> 00:02:40,190
to maintain it and if you need to frequently
update the system / the user-level programs

25
00:02:40,190 --> 00:02:45,879
this is also a good idea because you don't
need to recompile the entire kernel given that this model is

26
00:02:45,879 --> 00:02:50,640
now external to the microkernel

27
00:02:50,640 --> 00:02:57,620
it's also more secure because you run more
stuff in user mode so you have

28
00:02:57,620 --> 00:03:04,090
a smaller set of portions of code that
are the ones that can actually run privileged instructions

29
00:03:04,090 --> 00:03:06,360
so this makes it more secure

30
00:03:06,360 --> 00:03:11,430
but of course it might suffer in performance
if you have to manage the file system

31
00:03:11,430 --> 00:03:14,900
and every time you have to do something with
the disk you need to switch mode and give

32
00:03:14,900 --> 00:03:16,050
control of that microkernel

33
00:03:16,050 --> 00:03:28,049
you are wasting CPU cycles and this will affect
performance aside from microkernels you can

34
00:03:28,049 --> 00:03:37,430
have modules the idea is similar but the difference is that you
can load the modules inside the kernel 

35
00:03:37,430 --> 00:03:45,519
either at boot time or on demand and this
is what's commonly done and it's still

36
00:03:45,519 --> 00:03:48,830
done in the major operating systems we have today

37
00:03:48,830 --> 00:03:56,489
and you don't need to recompile the kernel
that's the important thing and finally what

38
00:03:56,489 --> 00:04:04,239
we actually have today is some kind of hybrid
system in which you take all the previous

39
00:04:04,239 --> 00:04:11,769
ones or at least you pick parts of the previous one depending
on what let's say to try to take all the things

40
00:04:11,769 --> 00:04:15,819
that were efficient depending on the case
and

41
00:04:15,819 --> 00:04:23,970
the system that was developed before here you
just to have some examples so if you look

42
00:04:23,970 --> 00:04:31,170
at Mac OS X you see that you have a layered
structure and then inside each layer you

43
00:04:31,170 --> 00:04:34,500
can have different parts different modules

44
00:04:34,500 --> 00:04:44,550
in the case of the OS X you have parts that
are from the unix system like sorry this one

45
00:04:44,550 --> 00:04:52,400
and parts that have
been developed directly for OS X the

46
00:04:52,400 --> 00:04:57,639
nice thing here with the layered structure
is that if you later on find out that you want

47
00:04:57,639 --> 00:05:02,410
to reuse your operating system for example
to port it

48
00:05:02,410 --> 00:05:07,720
to the iPhone as it was done in the beginning
when the first iPhone was

49
00:05:07,720 --> 00:05:13,160
released then you can keep your core layers
and keep adding new layers on top of it so

50
00:05:13,160 --> 00:05:19,520
for example a new layer that has to do with touch screen
that was not there before the iPhone

51
00:05:19,520 --> 00:05:29,750
and again here I'm showing you the OS X case but
if you look at the android one it looks very similar

52
00:05:29,750 --> 00:05:34,140
you have different layers and depending on
the layer then inside you have different

53
00:05:34,140 --> 00:05:34,659
modules

